<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.1.4: Describing Functional Relationships</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00849</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.1.4: Describing Functional Relationships</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d6f79b7-c69d-48fa-8c24-7e188f28df5c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>In a function, there are two quantities called <span data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">variables</span>. The <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">independent variable</term> is the input, while the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">dependent variable</term> is the output.</para>
<para>Here are two pairs of quantities from a situation you’ve seen in this lesson. Each pair has a relationship that
  can be defined as a function.</para>
<list>
  <item>Time, in seconds, since the dog owner left and the total number of times the dog has barked</item>
  <item>Time, in seconds, since the owner left and the total distance, in feet, that the dog has walked while waiting</item>
</list>
<para>Choose one pair of quantities and express their relationship as a function.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>In that function, which variable is independent? Which one is dependent?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>The time, in seconds, since the owner left is independent. The total number of times the dog has barked is
      dependent.</para>
    <para>The time, in seconds, since the owner left is independent. The total distance, in feet, the dog walked while
      waiting is dependent.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write a sentence of the form “____ is a function of ____.”</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    <para>The total number of times the dog has barked is a function of the time, in seconds, since the owner left.</para>
    <para>The total distance, in feet, is a function of the time, in seconds, since the owner left.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>On a piece of graph paper, sketch a possible graph that represents your chosen scenario.


      </para>
    <para>Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample for each of the scenarios.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ca4900cb37ad2bf12e8f0acdf90a1e1690f9bdff"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Time, in seconds, since the owner left and the total distance, in feet, that the dog has walked while waiting</para>
  <para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a969025a0b21f96106cdc152e08de402cf0a0557"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
